course: dictionary
lesson: 13793
title: Always, every time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Invariably means always, every time, without any change. If something happens invariably, it never changes its pattern. Look at the words around it for clues: «The bus is invariably late on rainy days.» If the bus is late every rainy day, invariably must mean always.
explain: Try the check. Swap in always: «The bus is always late on rainy days.» The sentence still makes sense, so you have found the meaning. Formal words like invariably often appear in stories and reports, so it helps to know them.
explain: Be careful with almost invariably. It means nearly always, but not every time. «The shop is almost invariably busy on Saturdays» means it is busy on most Saturdays, though maybe not every one. The word almost tells you there can be a few exceptions.
explain: Scientists use invariably for rules that never change. «Water invariably freezes at 0 degrees Celsius» means the rule holds every time. When you meet a formal word, read the sentence slowly, look for clues, and test your meaning by swapping in a simpler word.
fact: Invariably is an adverb. It tells you how often something happens.
fact: «Invariably» means always, every time, without any change.
fact: «Almost invariably» means nearly always, with very few exceptions.
fact: Invariably is a formal word, so you will often find it in stories, reports and science texts.
fact: In science, a rule that invariably holds is always true under the same conditions.
exercise: «The sun invariably rises in the east.» What does invariably mean here? | Sometimes, if the weather is good | Quickly, in a rush | *Always, every time | Very loudly
exercise: «The bus is almost invariably late on Mondays.» What does almost invariably mean? | Exactly twice a year | Just once | Never at all | *Nearly always
exercise: Dad says, «I invariably walk the dog at seven.» What does invariably tell us about Dad? | He does it only once | He does it only in winter | He does it very slowly | *He does it every time, without change
test: «Our teacher invariably starts the lesson with a quiz.» Which word could replace invariably without changing the meaning? | *always | rarely | sometimes | never
test: «Water invariably boils at 100 degrees at sea level.» What does this rule tell us? | It is true only for children | *It is always true | It is true only in winter | It is sometimes true
exam: «The café is almost invariably full on sunny days, so arrive early.» What does almost invariably mean? | Exactly once a year | Only once in a while | *Nearly always | Not at all
